% \title{\filebase}
% \author{Clea F. Rees}
% \date{\filedate}
% \maketitle
% \begin{abstract}
% An opentype font configuration package for Lua\LaTeXe{}.
% The aim is to provide an alternative to \pkg{fontspec} which is both faster
% and more powerful, by doing as much processing as possible in Lua.
% To avoid loading fonts unnecessarily, the data used is corrected during 
% compilation and the results cached for future use.
%
% \emph{This package is experimental.}
% \end{abstract}
% 
% \section{Requirements}\label{sec:require}
%
% The package requires Lua\LaTeXe.
% It will not work with any other engine or format.
%
% \section{Usage}\label{sec:pkg}
%
% \emph{The user interface is currently \textbf{extremely} basic.}
%
% All that is properly supported is the choice of font family for the standard
% families --- i.e.~roman/serif, sans and typewriter/mono --- and configuration
% of additional fonts, again by family.
% In addition to this, scaling and raw feature selection are supported.
% That is, most things are supported in the sense that they are possible, but
% the interface is rudimentary in the extreme.
%
% That said, the automated configuration is quite extensive.
% By default, the package tries to configure font families which are as rich
% as possible, insofar as the fonts support this. 
% This includes
% \begin{itemize}
%   \item multiple widths and shapes, insofar as \textsc{nfss} supports them;
%   \item italic, oblique, upright italic, reverse italic, reverse oblique;
%   \item small-caps, italic small-caps, oblique small-caps\footnote{%
%     This should work whether small-caps are in separate fonts (e.g.~Latin 
%     Modern) or implemented by the \texttt{smcp} feature (e.g.~\TeX{}-Gyre
%     Pagella).};
%   \item optical sizes.
% \end{itemize}
% Of course, this depends on the quality of the data the fonts provide.
% It is not always good or, even, vaguely accurate. 
% If things do not fit, an effort is made to accommodate them as distinct font
% families, so they are still available at document level.
%
% At present, this is a bit chaotic and a bit hard to use, as the 
% results are ‘hidden’ from you (in, of course, plain sight).
% Even so, you should typically need to do much less to access the fonts you
% want than you might otherwise expect. 
%
% The advantage of this is that if a font has several widths, you can easily
% adjust the one used for bold by simply redefining the relevant default.
% This is not very user friendly yet, but it is not terrible.
% 
%
% Simple usage looks like this:
% \iffalse
%<*verb>
% \fi
\begin{verbatim}
  \usepackage{lua-font-config}
  \fontconfig{%
    rm = texgyrepagella,
    sf = texgyreheros,
    tt = texgyrecursor,
  }
\end{verbatim}
% \iffalse
%</verb>
% \fi
% 
% If you want to configure font features, scale a family or use a non-default
% script etc., you can use:
% \iffalse
%<*verb>
% \fi
\begin{verbatim}
  \usepackage{lua-font-config}
  \fontconfig{%
    rm = {%
      name = texgyrepagella, 
      features = {script=ltn;+tlig;+pnum},
    },
    sf = {%
      name = texgyreheros,
      scale = 1.2,
      features = {script=cyrl;},
    },
    tt = {%
      name = texgyrecursor,
      features = {+zero;},
      %% for completeness: false is anyway the default
      force = false,
    },
  }
\end{verbatim}
% \iffalse
%</verb>
% \fi
% Do \textbf{not} specify small-capitals (\texttt{+smcp}) unless you want 
% \emph{all} fonts in the family to use that feature.
% Small-caps and italic/oblique small-caps will be configured 
% automatically if available.
%
% The only supported keys at present are
% \begin{description}
%   \item[name] The ‘name’ to lookup.
%     This may, but need not be, the name of a font family.
%     Shorter, less specific names will configure more families; longer, more specific names will configure fewer.
%     For example, \texttt{name = texgyrepagellax,} will configure fewer than \texttt{name = texgyrepagella,} since the latter string matches both families, while the former does not.
%     
%     The name is lower-cased prior to lookup. 
%     The data used to generate actual font families uses a cleaned up version of the given name or, sometimes, a variation, depending on the data returned from the database.
%     For example, \texttt{tex gyre PaGellA} yields the same results as \texttt{texgyrepagella}.
%     Numbers are likewise removed\footnote{%
%       This is problematic for fonts which depend on digits in their names to distinguish them e.g.~Venturis ADF has four distinct ‘titling’ fonts, whose names differ by a single character, which is \texttt{1}, \texttt{2}, \texttt{3} or \texttt{4}. 
%       The algorithm used to sort the fonts into \textsc{nfss} families currently gets confused by this, leading to misleading warnings about ‘apparent duplicate’ fonts. 
%       (Something is indeed awry, but they are not duplicates.
%       Rather what should be four families appears to be only one.)%
%     }
%   \item[fea] Optional list of font features. 
%       The default is \texttt{script=dflt}, \texttt{mode=node}, \texttt{+tlig}, \texttt{language=dflt}.
%   \item[scale] Optional factor by which the font should be scaled e.g.~\texttt{20} or \textt{0.8}.
%       Ignored if a family includes distinct optical sizes.
%   \item[force] Boolean which determines whether cached data is to be regenerated on the basis of a new search of the font-loader's database.
%       Note that this doesn't regenerate the loader's database itself. 
%       It simply zaps data stored by this module for one or more font families.
% \end{description}
%
\iffalse
%<*hmm>
% \section{Advanced Configuration}\label{sec:adv}
%
% Advanced configuration is possible, but the interface is currently non-
% existent and, despite not existing, will certainly change. 
%
% \iffalse
%<*verb>
% \fi
\begin{verbatim}
\directlua{
  lfc = require("lfc")
  local font_config = lfc.font_config
  font_config("venturis", {fea = "mode=node;script=dflt;+tlig;+pnum;+onum",
    scale=0.9, force = false}, false)
}
\end{verbatim}
% \iffalse
%</verb>
% \fi
%
% The function \texttt{font_config()} takes one mandatory and two optional arguments.
% The first is the string to look up, typically, but not necessarily, a family name.
% The second is a table of configuration data.
% The only used values are \texttt{fea}, \texttt{scale} and \texttt{force}, all explained in the previous section.
%
% The final \texttt{false} determines whether the definitions should be written immediately or code placed into a hook to be used on-demand.
% When the \LaTeX{} interface is used, this is set \texttt{true} for the main document families and \texttt{false} otherwise.
% Omitting it is equivalent to \texttt{false}.
%
% You do not actually gain much by this by used of the Lua interface.
% It may (or may not) be marginally faster, since key-value processing in \LaTeX{} is fairly slow. 
% However, you cannot actually do anything you cannot do otherwise, except for changing when and whether font definitions are actually used.
%
% So after the Lua above, you might do
% \iffalse
%<*verb>
% \fi
\begin{verbatim}
\renewcommand\rmdefault{venturisadf}
\renewcommand\sfdefault{venturissansadfcd}
\makeatletter
% TitlingNo3? (digits get eaten during lookup)
\DeclareRobustCommand\venturistitling{%
  \not@math@alphabet\venturistitling\relax
  \fontfamily{venturisadftitlingno}\selectfont
}
\DeclareTextFontCommand\textvtitle{\venturistitling}
\makeatother
\end{verbatim}
% \iffalse
%</verb>
% \fi
% Right now, the only way to know what is available is to examine the fonts
% or, better, the cached data (after the first run).
% You can also put \texttt{lfc = \{debug = true\}} before loading the module to 
% get (lots) of console output, including the database contents.
%
% That is, you can use the module directly, but that should not be understood
% to mean that the module is actually usable at present.
%
% Obviously, such shenanigans are not for the faint of heart or, indeed, for
% the saner portion of the stouter-hearted.
%
%</hmm>
\fi
